That's a choice. Let me qualify your statement:

Wizards cannot wear metal armor without giving up their considerable
spellcasting advantage. In addition because of wizards' natrually low
strength, armor will often burden a wizard making it heard for them to
function in the game.

You can wear metal armor. In the early game you SHOULD wear metal armor
as a wizard. Self preservation is more important than trying to maintain
a role in the early game. So BUC test (using your pet) and suit up. Even
if you end up wearing dwarven mithril, iron shoes, a metal helm, and
carrying a shield, the lowered AC will help in your quest to remain
alive until the true wizard inside can get fired up.

This is the primary reason I run the modified protection racket. It
facilitates replacing all the heavy metal armor that inhibits
spellcasting with divine protection which weighs nothing nor
impacts spellcasting at all. Coupled with the fact that in the later
stages of the game that highly enchanted dragon scale can be added to
the mix without impacting spellcasting either, along with magic
resistance you get from the jump, wizards can grow to be formidable in
the AC department.

To summarize, play a scared wizard initially. Let the familiar take on
the brunt of the monsters. Level up carefully. Get your AC down at all
costs until you can tackle stuff on your own.

